url fn n" href=3D"https://exploringnumbertheory.wordpress.com/author/explor=
ingnumbertheory/" title=3D"View all posts by Dan Ma" rel=3D"author">Dan Ma<=
/a></span></span>		</div><!-- .entry-meta -->
			</header><!-- .entry-header -->

	<div class=3D"entry-content">
		<p>This is the fourth post in a series of posts on the Chinese remainder =
theorem (usually abbreviated as CRT). <a href=3D"https://exploringnumberthe=
ory.wordpress.com/2015/11/15/how-to-chinese-remainder-part-1/" target=3D"_b=
lank">The previous post</a> presents an algorithm for solving systems of li=
near congruence equations, which is based on a constructive proof of CRT. T=
his post presents another algorithm that is based on another constructive p=
roof of CRT. Links to the previous posts in the series: <a href=3D"https://=
exploringnumbertheory.wordpress.com/2015/11/15/another-look-at-the-fundamen=
tal-theorem-of-arithmetic/" target=3D"_blank">first post</a>, <a href=3D"ht=
tps://exploringnumbertheory.wordpress.com/2015/11/15/versions-of-the-chines=
e-remainder-theorem/" target=3D"_blank">second post</a>, <a href=3D"https:/=
/exploringnumbertheory.wordpress.com/2015/11/15/how-to-chinese-remainder-pa=
rt-1/" target=3D"_blank">third post</a>.</p>
<p>The algorithm discussed here is to solve the following is the version of=
 the Chinese remainder theorem. This version has been shown to be equivalen=
t to several other versions in this previous post.</p>
<p><strong><em>Theorem G (Chinese Remainder Theorem)</em></strong><br>
Suppose <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dm_1%2Cm_2%2C%5Ccdot=
s%2Cm_t&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"=
m_1,m_2,\cdots,m_t" class=3D"latex"> are positive integers that are pairwis=
e relatively prime. Let <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dm%3=
Dm_1+%5Ccdot+m_2+%5Ccdots+m_t&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp=
;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"m=3Dm_1 \cdot m_2 \cdots m_t" class=3D"latex">. For a=
ny sequence of integers <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dc_1=
%2Cc_2%2C%5Ccdots%2Cc_t&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D2=
0201002" alt=3D"c_1,c_2,\cdots,c_t" class=3D"latex">, the following system =
of linear congruence equations</p>
<ul>
<img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dx+%5Cequiv+c_1+%5C+%28%5Cte=
xt%7Bmod%7D+%5C+m_1%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20=
201002" alt=3D"x \equiv c_1 \ (\text{mod} \ m_1)" class=3D"latex"><p></p>
<p><img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dx+%5Cequiv+c_2+%5C+%28%5=
Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+m_2%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=
=3D20201002" alt=3D"x \equiv c_2 \ (\text{mod} \ m_2)" class=3D"latex"></p>
<ul>
<img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3D%5Ccdots&amp;bg=3Dffffff&am=
p;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"\cdots" class=3D"latex"><p=
></p>
</ul>
