<p align=3D"center"> <em><strong>Discussion of Proof/Algorithm</strong></em=
> </p>
<p>To understand the algorithm, it is a good idea to understand the constru=
ctive proof of CRT on which the algorithm is based. The constructive proof =
is to build up a solution to the system of equations in CRT in an iterative=
 fashion. The first equation (stated in the above statement of CRT) has a s=
olution, which is <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dx_1%3Dc_1=
&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"x_1=3Dc=
_1" class=3D"latex">. Then the solution <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex=
.php?latex=3Dx_1&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002=
" alt=3D"x_1" class=3D"latex"> is used to build a solution to the first two=
 equations, say <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dx_2&amp;bg=
=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"x_2" class=3D"=
latex">, which is then used to build the solution to the first three equati=
ons, and so on. So this constructive proof of a solution is an inductive pr=
oof.</p>
<p>Let=E2=80=99s look at the case of two equations: <img src=3D"https://s0.=
wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dx+%5Cequiv+c_1+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+m_1%29&=
amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"x \equiv=
 c_1 \ (\text{mod} \ m_1)" class=3D"latex"> and <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.c=
om/latex.php?latex=3Dx+%5Cequiv+c_2+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+m_2%29&amp;=
bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"x \equiv c_2=
 \ (\text{mod} \ m_2)" class=3D"latex">. Then <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com=
/latex.php?latex=3Dx_1%3Dc_1&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;=
c=3D20201002" alt=3D"x_1=3Dc_1" class=3D"latex"> is the solution to the fir=
st equation. Express the equation <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?l=
atex=3Dx_1+%5Cequiv+c_1+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+m_1%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&=
amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"x_1 \equiv c_1 \ (\text{=
mod} \ m_1)" class=3D"latex"> as <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?la=
tex=3Dc_1%3Dx_1%2Bm_1+y&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D2=
0201002" alt=3D"c_1=3Dx_1+m_1 y" class=3D"latex"> for some integer <img src=
=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dy&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&a=
mp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"y" class=3D"latex">. We would like <img s=
rc=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dx_1%2Bm_1+y&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;f=
g=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"x_1+m_1 y" class=3D"latex"> t=
o be the solution to the second equation too. Then we need to solve for <im=
