x.php?latex=3Dm_j&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D2020100=
2" alt=3D"m_j" class=3D"latex">. The desired solution <img src=3D"https://s=
0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dx_t&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&am=
p;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"x_t" class=3D"latex"> may have to be reduced modulo =
<img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dm%3Dm_1+%5Ccdot+m_2+%5Ccdot=
s+m_%7Bt%7D&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=
=3D"m=3Dm_1 \cdot m_2 \cdots m_{t}" class=3D"latex">.</p>
<p>In a computer implementation, the algorithm in <img src=3D"https://s0.wp=
.com/latex.php?latex=3D%281%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&am=
p;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"(1)" class=3D"latex"> is carried out iteratively to =
find the solution. If the calculation is to be carried by hand as an exerci=
se, an alternative to memorizing the formula <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/=
latex.php?latex=3D%281%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=
=3D20201002" alt=3D"(1)" class=3D"latex"> is to know that each step <img sr=
c=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dx_%7Bj%7D&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=
=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"x_{j}" class=3D"latex"> is obt=
ained from solving for <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dy&am=
p;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"y" class=
=3D"latex"> in the following equation:</p>
<ul>
<img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dx_j+%5Cequiv+x_%7Bj-1%7D%2B=
m_1+%5Ccdot+m_2+%5Ccdots+m_%7Bj-1%7D+%5Ccdot+y+%5Cequiv+c_%7Bj%7D+%5C+%28%5=
Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+m_%7Bj%7D%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&a=
mp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"x_j \equiv x_{j-1}+m_1 \cdot m_2 \cdots m_{j-1} \cd=
ot y \equiv c_{j} \ (\text{mod} \ m_{j})" class=3D"latex">
</ul>
<p><strong><em>Comment</em></strong><br>
If there are <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dt&amp;bg=3Dfff=
fff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"t" class=3D"latex"> =
equations, the algorithm as described here requires the finding of <img src=
=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dt-1&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333=
&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"t-1" class=3D"latex"> applications of t=
he extended Euclidean algorithm (for the multiplicative inverse in each ind=
uction step). If the given equations are in the form <img src=3D"https://s0=
.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Da_i+%5Ccdot+x+%5Cequiv+b_i+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7=
D+%5C+m_i%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" al=
t=3D"a_i \cdot x \equiv b_i \ (\text{mod} \ m_i)" class=3D"latex">, then <i=
mg src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dt&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D33=
3333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"t" class=3D"latex"> additional appl=
ications of the extended Euclidean algorithm are required to convert the eq=
uations to the form <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dx+%5Ceq=
uiv+c_i+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+m_i%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&=
amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"x \equiv c_i \ (\text{mod} \ m_i)" class=
=3D"latex">. Overall, the amount of computation is comparable to the algori=
thm discussed in the previous post.</p>
<div class=3D"wordads-ad-wrapper" style=3D"display: inherit;"><div id=3D"at=
atags-26942-609657"></div></div><p>The iterative nature of this algorithm i=
s useful in the situations where the solution to a smaller system is known.=
 For example, if we add an additional equation to an existing system of equ=
ations, then the solution to the new system can be found based on the solut=
ion to the smaller system. In particular, with one additional application o=
f the extended Euclidean algorithm, the new solution can be found. The foll=
owing two examples demonstrate how this works.</p>
<p>________________________________________________________________________=
____</p>
<p align=3D"center"> <em><strong>Examples</strong></em> </p>
<p><strong><em>Example 1</em></strong><br>
Solve the following system of linear congruence equations.</p>
<ul>
<img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dx+%5Cequiv+3+%5C+%28%5Ctext=
%7Bmod%7D+%5C+17%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201=
002" alt=3D"x \equiv 3 \ (\text{mod} \ 17)" class=3D"latex"><p></p>
<p><img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dx+%5Cequiv+10+%5C+%28%5C=
text%7Bmod%7D+%5C+21%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D2=
0201002" alt=3D"x \equiv 10 \ (\text{mod} \ 21)" class=3D"latex"></p>
<p><img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dx+%5Cequiv+15+%5C+%28%5C=
text%7Bmod%7D+%5C+29%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D2=
0201002" alt=3D"x \equiv 15 \ (\text{mod} \ 29)" class=3D"latex"></p>
</ul>
<p>This is Example 1 from this previous post. The results are <img src=3D"h=
ttps://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dx_1%3D3&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&=
amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"x_1=3D3" class=3D"latex">, <img src=3D"h=
ttps://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dx_2%3D241&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D33333=
3&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"x_2=3D241" class=3D"latex"> and <img s=
rc=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dx_3%3D7381&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=
=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"x_3=3D7381" class=3D"latex"> a=
s derived below:</p>
<ul>
<p><img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3D%5Cdisplaystyle+%5Cbegin=
%7Baligned%7D+x_2%26%5Cequiv+3%2B17+%5Ccdot+5+%5Ccdot+%2810-3%29+%5C+%28%5C=
text%7Bmod%7D+%5C+17+%5Ccdot+21%29+%5C%5C%26%5Cequiv+598+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bm=
od%7D+%5C+357%29+%5C%5C%26%5Cequiv+241+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+357%29++=
%5Cend%7Baligned%7D&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201=
002" alt=3D"\displaystyle \begin{aligned} x_2&amp;\equiv 3+17 \cdot 5 \cdot=
 (10-3) \ (\text{mod} \ 17 \cdot 21) \\&amp;\equiv 598 \ (\text{mod} \ 357)=
 \\&amp;\equiv 241 \ (\text{mod} \ 357)  \end{aligned}" class=3D"latex"></p=
>
<p>where 5 is obtained by solving <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?l=
atex=3D17y+%5Cequiv+1+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+21%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp=
;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"17y \equiv 1 \ (\text{mod} =
\ 21)" class=3D"latex"></p>
<p><img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3D%5Cdisplaystyle+%5Cbegin=
%7Baligned%7D+x_3%26%5Cequiv+241%2B%2817+%5Ccdot+21%29+%5Ccdot+13+%5Ccdot+%=
2815-241%29+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+17+%5Ccdot+21+%5Ccdot+29%29+%5C%5C%=
26%5Cequiv+-1048625+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+10353%29+%5C%5C%26%5Cequiv+=
7381+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+10353%29++%5Cend%7Baligned%7D&amp;bg=3Dfff=
fff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"\displaystyle \begin=
{aligned} x_3&amp;\equiv 241+(17 \cdot 21) \cdot 13 \cdot (15-241) \ (\text=
{mod} \ 17 \cdot 21 \cdot 29) \\&amp;\equiv -1048625 \ (\text{mod} \ 10353)=
 \\&amp;\equiv 7381 \ (\text{mod} \ 10353)  \end{aligned}" class=3D"latex">=
</p>
<p>where 13 is obtained by solving <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?=
latex=3D%2817+%5Ccdot+21%29+y+%5Cequiv+1+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+29%29&=
amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"(17 \cdo=
t 21) y \equiv 1 \ (\text{mod} \ 29)" class=3D"latex"></p>
</ul>
<p>Example is now completed. <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=
=3D%5Csquare&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" al=
t=3D"\square" class=3D"latex"></p>
<p><strong><em>Example 2</em></strong><br>
To illustrate the iterative nature of the algorithm, we add an addition equ=
ation to the system in Example 1. Solve the following system of linear cong=
ruence equations.</p>
<ul>
<img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dx+%5Cequiv+3+%5C+%28%5Ctext=
%7Bmod%7D+%5C+17%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201=
002" alt=3D"x \equiv 3 \ (\text{mod} \ 17)" class=3D"latex"><p></p>
<p><img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dx+%5Cequiv+10+%5C+%28%5C=
text%7Bmod%7D+%5C+21%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D2=
0201002" alt=3D"x \equiv 10 \ (\text{mod} \ 21)" class=3D"latex"></p>
<p><img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dx+%5Cequiv+15+%5C+%28%5C=
text%7Bmod%7D+%5C+29%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D2=
0201002" alt=3D"x \equiv 15 \ (\text{mod} \ 29)" class=3D"latex"></p>
<p><img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dx+%5Cequiv+16+%5C+%28%5C=
text%7Bmod%7D+%5C+31%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D2=
0201002" alt=3D"x \equiv 16 \ (\text{mod} \ 31)" class=3D"latex"></p>
</ul>
<p>From Example 1, the solution to the first three equations is <img src=3D=
"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dx_3%3D7381+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C=
+10353%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=
=3D"x_3=3D7381 \ (\text{mod} \ 10353)" class=3D"latex"> where 10353 is the =
product of the first three moduli. The following gives the solution:</p>
<ul>
<img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3D%5Cdisplaystyle+%5Cbegin%7B=
aligned%7D+x_4%26%5Cequiv+7381%2B10353+%5Ccdot+30+%5Ccdot+%2816-7381%29+%5C=
+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+17+%5Ccdot+21+%5Ccdot+29+%5Ccdot+31%29+%5C%5C%26%5=
Cequiv+-2287487969+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+320943%29+%5C%5C%26%5Cequiv+=
193735+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+320943%29++%5Cend%7Baligned%7D&amp;bg=3D=
ffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"\displaystyle \be=
gin{aligned} x_4&amp;\equiv 7381+10353 \cdot 30 \cdot (16-7381) \ (\text{mo=
d} \ 17 \cdot 21 \cdot 29 \cdot 31) \\&amp;\equiv -2287487969 \ (\text{mod}=
 \ 320943) \\&amp;\equiv 193735 \ (\text{mod} \ 320943)  \end{aligned}" cla=
ss=3D"latex"><p></p>
<p>where 30 is obtained by solving <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?=
latex=3D10353+y+%5Cequiv+1+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+31%29&amp;bg=3Dfffff=
f&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"10353 y \equiv 1 \ (\t=
ext{mod} \ 31)" class=3D"latex"></p>
</ul>
<p>The above gives the unique solution to the given system of linear equati=
ons. <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3D%5Csquare&amp;bg=3Dfff=
fff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"\square" class=3D"la=
tex"></p>
